  "confidence": 0.55,
  "anomalies": [
    {
      "category": "wrong_value",
      "components": ["R53"],
      "pins": ["USB1.2"],
      "nets": ["USB_DM_FCC"],
      "observed": "On the Connections sheet, R53 = 560 Ω is wired in series between USB1 pin 2 (D-) and the net USB_DM_FCC. The matching D+ series resistor on the same connector is R52 = 22 Ω, and the identical USB2 (NAVC) connector uses 22 Ω on both data lines (R55 on D+, R56 on D-).",
      "expected": "A low series termination resistor (~22–33 Ω) on the USB D- line, consistent with R52 on D+ and with R55/R56 on USB2, and with standard USB high-speed signalling practice; 560 Ω is two orders of magnitude too large for a USB data line.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "Connections sheet: USB1 pin2 (D-) -> R53 560 -> USB_DM_FCC; USB1 pin3 (D+) -> R52 22 -> USB_DP_FCC; USB2 pin2 (D-) -> R56 22 -> USB_DM_NAVC"},
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "programming/debug via SWD, USB DFU, and UART-to-USB (spec gives no value for the USB series resistors, so standard practice applies)"}
      ],
      "impact": "The FCC's USB DFU / programming port is heavily attenuated on the D- line and will very likely fail to enumerate (or be unreliable), because a 560 Ω series resistor on a USB data line prevents proper signalling. The asymmetry with R52 (D+ = 22 Ω) and with the USB2 connector (both lines 22 Ω) indicates a mis-sourced/mis-assembled or tampered part rather than an intended value.",
      "confidence": 0.55
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The board otherwise matches the specification (correct regulators, shunt, crystal, sensor addresses, and the documented barometer RESET/EOC mix-up is a known bug, not reported). The one deviation the drawing supports is R53 = 560 Ω in series with the FCC USB D- line, where the D+ line and the companion USB2 connector both use 22 Ω; this would break FCC USB DFU/programming."
}

